  t("<b>Cripps offer: 4 provisions</b>", "<b>i) @Swaraj</b> <b>Dominion Status after war + Indian control of foreign policy</b> (Dominion was already in AO1940; now + foreign affairs); not Purna Swaraj", "<b>ii) @COI</b> <b>CA after war, solely Indians</b> (AO1940 said 'mainly'): <b>a) elected members of PLAs after fresh elections under GOI Act 1935; b) nominees of Princely States</b> (Rajya Sabha-like: indirect + nominated)", "<b>iii) @Unity</b> most explosive: once the CA frames the constitution, any <b>Province or Princely State that disagrees</b> may <b>not join the Union / secede</b> and frame its own constitution or union within the Empire", "<b>iv) @ToP</b> if power moved <b>during the war</b>, <b>Defence stays with the Viceroy</b>; no dilution of his powers over ministers; <b>he can veto any decision of ministers</b>", "<b>Talks actually broke on iv</b>: British 'take it or leave it' on the Viceroy's war powers", w=26),
  mn("Sir Cripps Unveiled Terms", "<b>S</b>waraj: Dominion + foreign policy · <b>C</b>OI: CA solely Indian · <b>U</b>nity: right to secede · <b>T</b>oP: Defence with the Viceroy <i>(order i to iv kept; my sentence)</i>"),
  bx('pk', "<b>Teacher's board on ii:</b> after GoI 1935 INC demanded a <b>UAF-elected CA</b>; the Cripps CA is <b>like the FA of GoI 1935: indirect elections in British India + nominees</b>. This is why INC later objected to Princely State nominees. <b>On iii:</b> teacher rewrites 'not join' as <b>'secede from'</b> and boxes <b>'framed'</b>: the option arises only once the constitution is framed. Steps: (1) CA frames constitution (2) members of a Province/PS pass a resolution of disagreement (3) it may secede (4) frames its own constitution or union. <b>On step 1: majority vote, no veto, so a united India is created</b>: contrast with AO1940's veto, and then the secession clause breaks it up."),
